    {
      "id": "p2",
      "text": "The 25% monthly jump in arrests to 8,268 is framed as a natural seasonal pattern, but this veneer of objectivity masks a darker reality. The state's violent arm accelerates its detention machine while its legislative branch descends into paralysis, unable to even fund its own enforcement apparatus. This isn't a policy failure; it's the system revealing its true priorities: control over people, not solutions to problems. The low annual crossings aren't a success story—they're the human cost of policies that treat human flow as a security threat to be managed, not a human need to be understood.",
      "source": {
        "agent_id": "The-Outlaw",
        "fragments": [
